# A whitelist-style .gitignore: block everything at the root, then re-open
# exactly what belongs to the project.
#
# Why the inversion: the root of an Xcode project is where tools drop things by
# themselves — DerivedData, SwiftPM's .build, working directories for assorted
# assistants, screenshots, throwaway builds. A blocklist always trails reality:
# anything nobody has hit yet has no line blocking it, and it lands in a commit.
# A whitelist fails in the safer direction — add a real directory to the project
# and forget to declare it here and `git status` goes quiet, but no junk ever
# works its way into history.
#
# Adding a new top-level directory means adding a `!/name/` line below.

/*

!/.gitignore
!/CHANGELOG.md
!/CLAUDE.md
!/LICENSE
!/README.md

!/Caffeinate/
!/Caffeinate.xcodeproj/
!/CaffeinateKit/
!/CaffeinateUITests/
!/Configs/
!/Scripts/
!/docs/

# Inside the re-opened directories, block the generated parts. The whitelist
# above only applies at the root level, so these still have to be named.

.build/
.swiftpm/
DerivedData/
*.xcuserstate
xcuserdata/
.DS_Store
